-- BASELINE — what the 204 files in `migrations-legacy/` build, as of 2026-09-10: the schema,
-- the rows those files seeded, and the privileges they granted.
--
-- Generated by CI (`quality.yml` -> migrations -> "Publish the baseline the legacy migrations
-- build"), never by hand and never dumped from production: a hand-edit made in Studio would
-- otherwise be baked in and silently become the definition. "The baseline is what the legacy
-- migrations produce" applies the legacy set to one database and this file to a fresh one,
-- and requires the two to agree on schema, privileges and seeded rows.
--
-- THE SEEDED ROWS ARE INSERTS, NOT COPY BLOCKS. On a new database `supabase db push` applies
-- this file statement by statement, and `COPY … FROM stdin` is not a statement: it needs the
-- client to stream the rows that follow it.
--
-- WHAT A DUMP OF THREE SCHEMAS CANNOT CARRY is declared elsewhere: the two roles it grants
-- to, and pg_cron, are created by `before-migrations.sql` before `db push` runs, and the
-- pg_cron jobs the legacy files scheduled are declared by migration 20261006210000.
--
-- NOT INCLUDED, DELIBERATELY: `always/001-app.sql` and `always/003-security.sql`. Both are
-- idempotent by design and must run on EVERY deploy — `003` revokes the DEFAULT PRIVILEGES so
-- a table LangGraph creates later cannot silently re-acquire anon access, which only works
-- because it re-runs. Baselining them would stop that.
